{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## THE MAP'S STALENESS IS NOW CHECKABLE — Gerrit 1394\n\nWorking this ticket under `/rebar-implement`'s held-out TDD discipline. The skill's Phase-0 gate\n**stopped**: 259b has no children (`rebar list-descendants` → all buckets empty), and the skill\nexecutes an existing decomposition rather than creating one. Implemented directly against 259b's\nown ACs with the same discipline instead — tests authored first, held out from the implementer.\n\n### The gap I found, and why it is the one that mattered\n\nAuditing the ACs against `origin/main` rather than against the ticket's prose: **AC1, AC2, AC3,\nAC5 and AC6 are already met** by changes 1328/1333 and by the falsifier comments now on 37e7 and\n1a9f. **AC4's consumption half is met too** — but by [rebar:3fe5-c22e-dc2d-4c9e], not by this\nticket: `_assert_project_capabilities` in `tests/external/live_jira_dc/conftest.py` enforces the\ndeclared `_PROJECT_TEMPLATE` / `_REQUIRED_FIELDS` contract before any test runs. This doc still\ndescribed that as prospective work (\"*an updated `docs/jira-dc-value-map.md` or* a harness setup\nassertion that reads it\"), naming a file that was never created; corrected to point at what\nactually landed.\n\nWhat was NOT met was implied by this ticket's premise rather than by a numbered AC. **The map\ncould go stale without anything noticing**, in two ways:\n\n1. **The artifact's provenance was a hand-copied constant.** `_HARNESS_IMAGE_DIGEST` in\n   `scripts/jira_dc_capability_map.py` duplicated the digest pinned on\n   `tests/external/live_jira_dc/Dockerfile`'s `FROM` line — while the comment directly above it\n   claimed the opposite: *\"pinned by construction, not by a second literal that could drift from\n   the first.\"* It **was** that second literal. The two agree today and **no test compared\n   them**, so the first re-pin would have left `run_metadata.json` reporting the OLD digest —\n   mislabelling which image was mapped, in the one file whose entire job is provenance.\n2. **The committed answers named no image at all.** This doc warned that \"a stale map after a\n   re-pin describes an image nothing runs anymore\" while recording nothing that could be checked\n   against — an unfalsifiable warning. And since the instance **does not expose its own digest**\n   over REST (only `scmInfo`), no live check could have caught it either.\n\n### What landed\n\n`harness_image_digest()` reads the `FROM` line, so the reported digest cannot drift from the\nimage actually built. It deliberately **does not raise** — this is metadata about a long,\nbillable live run, and aborting one over a malformed pin would discard the evidence the run\nexists to collect; a missing file or an undigested `FROM` reports \"no digest\" rather than falling\nback to a remembered value, which would mislabel the artifact just as badly as the bug it\nreplaces. The measured digest is now **committed data in this doc, asserted against the\nDockerfile's pin**, so a re-pin lands red until the map is regenerated. Plus `--print-digest`:\nwhich image a run *would* map, with no container, key, or LLM.\n\n### TDD evidence\n\nTests RED first, and I had to fix the oracle once before trusting it — the first run failed with\n`ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'rebar_reconciler.adapters'`, i.e. **the wrong reason**.\nRoot cause: `tests/unit/` ships its own `rebar_reconciler/` package and pytest puts the test's own\ndirectory on `sys.path` ahead of the engine, so an in-process import of the script resolves the\nshadowing test package. Rewrote the cells to drive the **real CLI** in a subprocess — which also\ncovers a gap worth naming: **`make lint` only lints `src tests`, so nothing in the local gate\nreads `scripts/` at all**; a break there would first surface partway into a billable live run.\n\nThe implementer subagent saw **only** the happy-path cell (reported digest == Dockerfile digest);\nthe re-pin case, the unpinned case, and the doc staleness gate were physically moved out of the\nworktree. **Both withheld edge cells passed unseen** — real signal, because a hardcoded constant\npasses the happy path and still fails the re-pin case. Confirmed with teeth: re-introducing the\nhardcoded digest turns exactly those 2 held-out cells RED while the happy path stays green.\n\nGates: `make lint` 0 (ruff + actionlint + zizmor), `make typecheck` 0 (mypy, 432 files),\n12/12 cells in the target file, 96/96 across the repo-wide gates (docs-index, module-size,\ncomment-hygiene, workflow-parity/names, repo-isolation). `scripts/` checked directly since lint\nskips it: `py_compile` 0, `--help` 0, `--print-digest` 0 emitting the Dockerfile's exact digest.\nThe full `tests/unit` suite does not finish in a usable local window — CI's `Verified` is the\nauthority there.\n\n### The residual gap, filed as [rebar:a9bd-6641-e603-42bc]\n\nThis gate keys on the **digest**, so it catches \"these answers describe a different image\". It\ndoes **not** catch **\"these answers are wrong about THIS image\"** — which is the failure this\nepic actually hit: the retracted 255-char label claim was wrong at an **unchanged** digest. The\nmechanism is that the answers are **LLM-summarised**; `evidence.json` was right and the *reading*\nof it was wrong. That is the trust boundary this ticket set out to remove (\"we have swapped\n'discovering from the instance' for 'trusting a model', which is worse\"), and closing it needs a\ndeterministic, non-LLM re-measurement pass. Filed with the description/comment 32767 **coverage\ngap** (limit confirmed as configured, never as observed) recorded alongside it.\n\nNot closing this ticket — the operator coordinating the epic is verifying the ACs."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785884104220780001,"uuid":"c8f2ce66-f34e-4e4f-9062-7becdfec820b"}